## Updated, more economical strategy

Below is a concise rule‑based controller that keeps the good parts of the current policy (full forward thrust when we already have forward momentum) but trims the unnecessary force close to the goal and softens the reverse thrust when the car is stuck.

```
IF p >= 0.45                     THEN a = 0.0                     # goal reached – coast

ELSE IF p >= 0.35 AND v > 0      THEN a = 0.3                     # coasting zone, keep a little push

ELSE IF v > 0                    THEN a = 1.0                     # already moving forward – max thrust

ELSE IF -0.05 <= v <= 0.05 AND p < -0.6
                                 THEN a = 0.9                     # deep left, no speed – strong nudge

ELSE IF -0.05 <= v <= 0.05 AND -0.6 <= p < -0.2
                                 THEN a = 0.6                     # left‑mid, no speed – moderate nudge

ELSE IF -0.05 <= v <= 0.05 AND -0.2 <= p < 0.0
                                 THEN a = 0.3                     # right‑mid, no speed – light nudge

ELSE IF v < 0 AND p > -0.4       THEN a = -0.5                    # moving left on the right side – gentle brake

ELSE IF v < 0 AND p <= -0.4      THEN a = 0.2                     # moving left deep in the valley – let gravity help, give a tiny forward push

ELSE                                 a = 0.0                     # catch‑all (should rarely fire)
```

### Why this works

| Condition | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|
| `p >= 0.45 → a = 0` | No more control cost after the reward is collected. |
| `0.35 ≤ p < 0.45 & v > 0 → a = 0.3` | Keeps the car climbing but with a much smaller penalty than `a = 1`. |
| `v > 0 → a = 1` | Maximal forward thrust is the most efficient way to convert the current kinetic energy into additional speed. |
| “Zero‑velocity & left‑side” blocks (`|v| ≤ 0.05`) | Replicates the successful “position‑dependent kick” from the current best policy, but only triggers when the car is truly stalled. |
| `v < 0 & p > -0.4 → a = -0.5` | When the car is already moving left on the right side of the valley we only need a *gentle* brake; a full `‑1` would waste extra penalty. |
| `v < 0 & p ≤ -0.4 → a = 0.2` | Deep in the left valley a strong reverse thrust is counter‑productive – a tiny forward nudge lets the car roll back forward after a few oscillations without paying a large ‑0.1 a² cost. |
| Catch‑all `a = 0` | Safety net for any state that slips through the explicit conditions. |

### Expected impact

* **Higher average reward** – by reducing the thrust magnitude in the coasting zone and when braking, the cumulative `‑0.1 a²` penalty should drop by roughly 0.5 – 1.0 points per episode while still reaching the goal in a comparable number of steps.  
* **More stable trajectories** – the gentle reverse thrust prevents the car from “wiggling” at the left hill and reduces the chance of getting stuck in a limit‑cycle of tiny forward/backward pushes.  
* **Preserves the core advantage** – the policy still exploits the fact that, once the car has any forward velocity, the fastest way to the top is to keep pushing hard.

