## Proposed “best‑so‑far” policy

Below is a **single deterministic rule set** that combines the insights above.  
It is written in the same pseudo‑code style you have been using, but you can also implement the compact linear‑feedback version if you prefer.

```text
# --------------------------------------------------------------
# Mountain‑Car policy – aim: reach position >= 0.45 as fast as possible
# --------------------------------------------------------------

# constants (tuned by hand)
GOAL_POS      = 0.45
RAMP_START    = 0.43          # start soft‑landing a few meters before the top
K_POS         = 50.0          # gain for position error (used only in the ramp)
K_VEL         = 10.0          # gain for velocity error (used only in the ramp)
FULL_THROTTLE = 1.0
BRAKE_THROTTLE= -1.0
MIN_POS_FOR_RAMP = 0.2        # below this we stay at full throttle

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Goal reached → no more thrust (the episode will terminate)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
IF position >= GOAL_POS THEN
    action = 0.0

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. We are already moving forward (positive velocity)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF velocity > 0.001 THEN

    # 2a. Close to the top → start a smooth ramp down
    IF position >= RAMP_START THEN
        # Desired acceleration that would bring us exactly to the goal
        # while still keeping a small forward speed (≈0.04 m/s)
        err_pos   = GOAL_POS - position          # > 0 but small
        err_vel   = 0.07 - velocity               # we want ~0.07 m/s at the top
        action = max(0.0,
                     K_POS * err_pos + K_VEL * err_vel)
    # 2b. Still far from the top → full forward thrust
    ELSE
        action = FULL_THROTTLE
    ENDIF

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. We are moving backwards (negative velocity) → brake hard
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF velocity < -0.001 THEN
    action = BRAKE_THROTTLE

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Velocity is (practically) zero – decide based on position only
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
ELSE
    IF position < -0.6 THEN
        action = 0.9               # still deep in the left valley → strong push
    ELSIF position < -0.2 THEN
        action = 0.6
    ELSIF position < 0.0 THEN
        action = 0.3
    ELSIF position < MIN_POS_FOR_RAMP THEN
        action = 0.1               # already on the right slope, but not yet in ramp
    ELSE
        # we are on the right slope but not yet in the ramp zone
        action = 0.05
    ENDIF
ENDIF

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Clamp to the allowed action range
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
IF action > 1.0  THEN action = 1.0
IF action < -1.0 THEN action = -1.0
```

### Why this should be the **most effective** policy

| Situation                              | What the rule does | Why it helps |
|----------------------------------------|--------------------|--------------|
| **Deep left valley (pos < ‑0.6)**       | `action = 0.9` (almost full) | Maximises the energy that can be stored in the valley without hitting the ‑1 limit (‑1 would be wasteful when we are still far from the goal). |
| **Mid‑valley (‑0.6 ≤ pos < ‑0.2)**      | `action = 0.6`     | Still strong enough to keep the momentum building, but a little softer to reduce the ‑0.1·a² penalty. |
| **Near the bottom (‑0.2 ≤ pos < 0)**    | `action = 0.3`     | The car already has a decent speed; a modest push is sufficient and saves reward. |
| **Right slope, still far from goal**   | `action = 0.1` / `0.05` | Prevents overshooting the hill while the car is already moving forward. |
| **Positive velocity, far from the top**| `action = 1.0`     | Keeps the car accelerating up the right hill – the fastest way to reach the goal. |
| **Positive velocity, close to the top**| **ramp** (`K_POS·err_pos + K_VEL·err_vel`) | Provides a smooth deceleration that leaves a small forward speed (~0.07 m/s) at the goal, exactly what the successful traces showed. |
| **Negative velocity**                  | `action = -1.0`    | Acts as a brake, quickly reversing the drift back into the valley. |

The **ramp** replaces the ad‑hoc “max(0, 50·(0.45‑pos)+10·(0.07‑vel))” that you already tried, but it is now **only activated** when the car is already moving forward and is inside the last 0.02 m of the hill. This eliminates the occasional “overshoot‑then‑brake” that caused a few ‑99.9 episodes.

